  "problem": "Write $-18{s}^{2}-32$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$-18{s}^{2}-32=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's factor out $-2$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n-18{s}^{2}-32=-2\\,(9{s}^{2}+16).\n$$\n\nNow focus on factoring $(9{s}^{2}+16).$  \n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor it as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{s}^{2}+16 & =9{s}^{2}-(-16) \\\\  & =9{s}^{2}-(16{i}^{2}) \\\\  & =(3s)^{2}-(4i)^{2} \\\\  & =(3s+4i)(3s-4i).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back, we obtain\n\n$$\n-2\\,(9{s}^{2}+16)=-2(3s+4i)(3s-4i).\n$$",
